<PageTitle>Philosophy &amp; History — QuickResult</PageTitle>

<article class="doc-page">
    <h1>Philosophy &amp; History of the Result Pattern</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        The Result pattern is older than most languages that use it. Understanding where it came from
        makes it obvious why it belongs in modern C#.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem: two kinds of failure, one mechanism</h2>
    <p>
        Every program deals with two very different kinds of failure. <strong>Exceptional failures</strong> —
        out of memory, corrupted state, broken invariants — are things the current operation cannot
        meaningfully respond to. <strong>Expected failures</strong> — a user typed an invalid email, an
        account wasn't found, a payment was declined — are ordinary, anticipated outcomes of the domain.
    </p>
    <p>
        Exceptions are a good tool for the first kind and a poor one for the second. When expected
        failures travel as exceptions, four things go wrong:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The signature lies.</strong> <code>Customer GetCustomer(string id)</code> claims to
            always return a customer. The failure mode is invisible until it detonates at runtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Control flow becomes non-local.</strong> A throw is a hidden goto — the reader can't
            see where handling happens without tracing every caller.</li>
        <li><strong>Handling is optional.</strong> Nothing forces a caller to catch. Forgotten catches
            are found in production.</li>
        <li><strong>It's slow.</strong> Stack unwinding costs orders of magnitude more than returning a
            value — a real tax when "failure" happens thousands of times per minute as normal business.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The Result pattern's answer: <em>make failure a value</em>. A function that can fail returns a
        type that says so — <code>Result&lt;Customer&gt;</code> — and the type system makes handling it
        as unavoidable as using the value itself. The signature becomes honest, the failure path becomes
        visible, and the compiler becomes your reviewer.
    </p>

    <h2>A brief history</h2>
    <dl class="history-list">
        <dt>1960s–70s — error codes</dt>
        <dd>
            C returned integers and set <code>errno</code>. Failure-as-value, but untyped, easily
            ignored, and disconnected from the value it accompanied. The right instinct without the
            type system to enforce it.
        </dd>
        <dt>1980s–90s — sum types and <em>Either</em></dt>
        <dd>
            ML-family languages introduced algebraic data types: a value that is exactly one of several
            tagged alternatives. Haskell's <code>Maybe</code> and <code>Either e a</code> made
            "value or error" a first-class, pattern-matchable type — and its monadic composition
            (<code>do</code> notation, 1990s) solved the ergonomics: chain computations and let the
            first failure short-circuit. Every modern Result library is a descendant of this idea —
            including the LINQ query you write with QuickResult, which is C#'s closest cousin to
            <code>do</code> notation.
        </dd>
        <dt>2003 — checked exceptions cautionary tale</dt>
        <dd>
            Java tried to force failure-handling through the type system with checked exceptions. The
            goal was right; the mechanism fought the language (no composition, no generics integration)
            and taught the industry that failure contracts must <em>compose</em> to be usable.
        </dd>
        <dt>2010s — Rust makes Result mainstream</dt>
        <dd>
            Rust shipped without exceptions: <code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> and the <code>?</code>
            operator are <em>the</em> error mechanism. A systems language proved that failure-as-value
            scales to entire ecosystems, and a generation of developers came to expect it.
        </dd>
        <dt>2013 — "Railway-Oriented Programming"</dt>
        <dd>
            Scott Wlaschin's F# talk gave the pattern its most durable metaphor: a function is a piece
            of two-track railway. Success rides the top track; the first failure switches the train to
            the bottom track, which bypasses every later step. F# later added a built-in
            <code>Result</code> type (F# 4.1, 2017).
        </dd>
        <dt>2015–present — the C# ecosystem</dt>
        <dd>
            C# gained the ingredients — expression-bodied members, pattern matching, records, nullable
            reference types — and a family of Result libraries grew: LanguageExt, CSharpFunctionalExtensions,
            FluentResults, ErrorOr. Each picks a point on the spectrum between "full functional toolkit"
            and "minimal result type".
        </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Where QuickResult sits</h2>
    <p>
        QuickResult stakes out a deliberate position in that spectrum:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>LINQ-first.</strong> C# already ships a monadic composition syntax — query
            expressions. Rather than inventing a fluent DSL, QuickResult teaches <code>Result</code>
            to speak it, including the part other libraries stumble on: <em>mixing sync and async
            steps in one query</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Minimal, not maximal.</strong> One namespace, a dozen public types, zero
            dependencies. It's a Result type, not a functional-programming worldview — you can adopt
            it in one method of a legacy codebase this afternoon.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Pragmatic at the edges.</strong> Real .NET code meets exceptions and nulls
            constantly, so <code>Try</code>, <code>TryAsync</code>, <code>FromNullable</code>, and
            <code>WhenNull</code> are front-door APIs, not afterthoughts.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Exceptions still exist.</strong> QuickResult doesn't abolish exceptions — it
            reserves them for what they're good at: the truly exceptional. Broken invariants should
            still throw. Declined payments should not.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The test</h2>
    <p>
        A simple heuristic for choosing between a Result and an exception: <em>would you write this
        failure into the user story?</em> "When the account is not found, show a message" — that's
        domain behavior, and it deserves a place in the type signature. "When the heap is exhausted…" —
        nobody writes that story. Let it throw.
    </p>

